---
import FaqLanding from '../../components/FaqLanding.astro';

const faqs = [
	{
		q: 'Our bank is already supervised by the BSP. Do we also answer to the NPC?',
		a: '<strong>Yes. They regulate different things.</strong> The BSP supervises you as a financial institution: the MORB, IT risk, consumer protection. The National Privacy Commission (NPC) regulates how you handle personal data under the Data Privacy Act (RA 10173). The DPA exempts bank processing done to comply with the AMLA and the Credit Information System Act, but only that processing, never the bank as a whole. A BSP-compliant bank can still be penalised by the NPC, so build one privacy program that satisfies both.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Does a bank legally need a Data Protection Officer?',
		a: '<strong>Yes, as does every organization that processes personal data.</strong> The duty has no size threshold (IRR Section 26(a); NPC Advisory 2017-01). For a bank the stakes sit underneath: the government-issued IDs in your KYC files are sensitive personal information (DPA Section 3(l)(3)), and credit scoring is profiling, which must be registered at any size. The DPO is also the registered contact the NPC deals with.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What happens if customer financial data is breached?',
		a: '<strong>Two clocks start.</strong> If the data could enable fraud, such as account numbers, balances or IDs, you must notify the NPC and the affected clients within 72 hours of learning of the breach, and file a full report within five days (NPC Circular 16-03). The BSP has its own reporting rules on top. Late or missing notification is a separate violation, fined at up to 2% of gross income, and concealing a breach is a criminal offence.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Do we have to register with the NPC, and how often?',
		a: '<strong>Almost certainly yes, and the certificate must be renewed every year.</strong> Registration is triggered by any one of four grounds (NPC Circular 2022-04, Section 5): 250 or more employees; sensitive personal information of 1,000 or more individuals; processing likely to pose a risk to rights and freedoms; or automated decision-making and profiling at any size. Credit scoring alone puts most banks over the last one. The certificate is valid for one year and can be renewed only in the 30 days before it expires. Miss that window and you are treated as unregistered.',
	},
	{
		q: 'We use third-party processors (core banking, e-KYC, collections). Are we still liable?',
		a: '<strong>Yes. Outsourcing the work does not outsource the accountability.</strong> As the Personal Information Controller, the bank remains answerable for data handled by its vendors. That exposure is managed through outsourcing and data-sharing agreements, breach-reporting clauses, and vendor due diligence, all of which the DPO oversees.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What can NPC penalties actually cost us?',
		a: '<strong>Two separate regimes, and they stack with BSP sanctions.</strong> NPC administrative fines run 0.5% to 3% of prior-year gross income for a grave infraction and 0.25% to 2% for a major one, capped at ₱5 million for any single act. Failure to register is ₱50,000 to ₱200,000 per infraction. The DPA’s criminal provisions are separate, carry six months to seven years’ imprisonment, and fall on responsible officers personally. The BSP can sanction the same lapse under RA 11765. For a bank, a compliant privacy program costs a fraction of one enforcement action.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Can you act as our external DPO?',
		a: '<strong>Yes.</strong> As a TÜV Certified DPO (Cert #7000532) based in Region 8, I can serve as your designated external DPO, build your Privacy Management Program, train your staff, and be your point of contact with the NPC, without the cost of a full-time hire. As a lawyer, what you disclose to me is also protected by attorney-client privilege.',
	},
];
---

